      "math_academy_id": "q-38046",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=6v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=6.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
